---
import CaseStudy from "../../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Brief from "../../../components/case/Brief.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../../components/case/CaseSection.astro";
import Diagram from "../../../components/case/Diagram.astro";
import MeasuresDetail from "../../../components/case/MeasuresDetail.astro";
import MeasuresTable from "../../../components/case/MeasuresTable.astro";
import Limit from "../../../components/case/Limit.astro";
import Figure from "../../../components/Figure.astro";
import { measure } from "../../../lib/measures.ts";
import { pinned } from "../../../lib/sources.ts";
import answer from "../../../data/demo/answer.json";

const at = (path: string) => pinned("verity", path);
// The run of record of the release the figures come from; the demo replays an earlier test run.
// Hashes stay in the links, out of the prose.
const runOfRecord = at("datasets/eval/runs/live/eval_513b839_test.incomplete-20261107T122042441670Z.json");
const demoRun = answer.source.url;

// Both quotes come from data that npm run check:sources compares with GitHub: the cited
// passage from the committed run (demo/answer.json), the threshold sentence from the
// evidence of its measure.
const DEFECT = "twentyfour";
const cited = answer.citations.find((c) => c.quote.includes(DEFECT));
if (!cited) throw new Error(`no citation of the demo answer contains "${DEFECT}"`);
const [before, after] = cited.quote.split(DEFECT) as [string, string];
const thresholdSentence = measure("verity.threshold_run_of_record").evidence.find((e) => "contains" in e && e.contains.startsWith("The threshold decides"));
if (!thresholdSentence || !("contains" in thresholdSentence)) throw new Error("the threshold sentence is not in the measure's evidence");
---

<CaseStudy
  no="01"
  name="Verity"
  repo="verity"
  serif
  summary="An agent that answers questions about contracts by quoting the clause word for word, and refuses when it has no evidence."
  description="Case study of Verity, an agent that answers about contracts by citing its sources: problem, architecture, results with their uncertainty, defects, decisions."
>
  <Brief
    slot="brief"
    does="It answers questions about a set of contracts by quoting the exact clause, and says “I don’t know” when the documents do not contain the answer."
    measured="On test questions never seen during tuning: whether it refuses the unanswerable questions, whether its answers make anything up, and whether it does better than the same chain with no checks."
    defect="It still answers some questions that have no answer in the documents; the questions involved are named in the published results."
    figures={[
      { id: "verity.invented_answer_rate", caption: "answers containing a fabrication, over every answer given, whether the question has an answer or not (according to the judge)" },
      { id: "verity.refusal_recall", caption: "unanswerable questions correctly refused" },
      { id: "verity.unanswerable_answered", caption: "unanswerable questions it answered anyway", defect: true },
    ]}
  />

  <CaseSection n={1} id="probleme" title="The problem" lead="In three sentences, why this project exists.">
    <p>
      Assistants that answer over company documents do so with confidence, and are wrong just often enough to be dangerous. On a contract, a fluent but wrong answer costs more than an “I don’t
      know”. Verity answers only by quoting the clause word for word, refuses otherwise, and measures whether it refuses correctly.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    n={2}
    id="construit"
    title="What I built"
    lead="The path of a question, from finding the passages to the answer or the refusal: it shows where a refusal is decided."
  >
    <Diagram
      id="verity-pipeline"
      title="The path of a question through Verity"
      steps={[
        { title: "Question", lines: ["over a corpus of contracts and policies"] },
        { title: "Passage search", lines: ["by meaning and by words,", "then ranking of the most useful passages"] },
        { title: "Drafting with quotes", lines: ["each quote must be an exact excerpt", "of the document; no quote, refusal"] },
        { title: "Checks in code, no model", lines: ["quotes: do they cover the answer?", "manipulation?", "a single failure refuses"] },
        { title: "Confidence rating and threshold", lines: ["a model rates the answer and may refuse;", "it cannot overrule a check"] },
      ]}
      outcomes={[
        { title: "Cited answer", lines: ["clause and position"], tone: "proof" },
        { title: "Refusal", lines: ["with its cause", "recorded"], tone: "defect" },
      ]}
      desc="A question is asked over a corpus of contracts and policies. Verity searches for passages by meaning and by words, then ranks the most useful ones. It drafts an answer in which each quote must be an exact excerpt of the document; with no quote, it refuses. Checks written in code, with no model, verify that the quotes cover the answer and that nothing manipulates them; a single failure refuses. Finally, a model rates the answer and may refuse below a threshold, but cannot overrule a check. Two outcomes: an answer citing its clause and position, or a refusal with its cause recorded."
      caption="The refusal is decided in order: no quote, then the checks written in code, then the confidence rating and its threshold. The judge, a second model that rates the answers, is not on this path: it evaluates the runs afterwards."
    />
    <p>
      Verity is a RAG agent: it first looks for the useful passages in the documents, then drafts its answer from them. It keeps the exact position of each passage in its document and returns either
      an answer whose every quote is found as is in the source, or a refusal with its cause. The demo on the <a href="/en/">home page</a> replays answers from a real run.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    n={3}
    id="mesures"
    title="Results"
    lead="What its answers and refusals are worth, in figures, with their uncertainty and a comparison with a simpler system."
  >
    <p>
      The figures come from the <a href={runOfRecord}>reference run</a> of the published release, on test questions never seen during tuning; the spending cap stopped it before the last two
      questions, which count nowhere. On the questions whose documents do not contain the answer, Verity refuses <Figure id="verity.refusal_recall" where="prose" /> of them, but still answers <Figure
        id="verity.unanswerable_answered"
        where="prose"
        defect
      />. The judge, a second model in charge of rating the answers, finds a fabrication in <Figure id="verity.invented_answer_rate" where="prose" /> answers given (<code>q-089</code>), every question
      counted. The earlier definition counted only the questions that have an answer: by construction it left out the one real fabrication of the previous run (<code>q-074</code>). It gives <Figure
        id="verity.hallucination_rate"
        where="prose"
      /> here and stays published in the detail, with its definition.
    </p>
    <p>
      This judge agrees with my own ratings, made blind to the judge’s, at <Figure id="verity.judge_kappa_v020" where="prose" />, on part of the validation claims only: its budget stopped the check before
      the end. That is a Cohen’s kappa, a measure of agreement that removes the share due to chance, from zero for chance to one for perfect agreement. The earlier judge prompt, the one of the previous
      runs, reached <Figure id="verity.judge_kappa_all" where="prose" /> on claims of which only one is a real hallucination; its interval is in the detail.
    </p>
    <p>
      Against a naive RAG, the same search and the same drafting with no check at all, the comparison was pre-registered and covers the tuning and test questions both finished. Verity handles <Figure
        id="verity.twoarm_unanswerable_answered_verity"
        where="prose"
      /> unanswerable questions as if they had an answer, against <Figure id="verity.twoarm_unanswerable_answered_naive" where="prose" /> for the naive RAG: a clear gap (paired test, adjusted for three
      comparisons: p = <Figure id="verity.twoarm_unanswerable_answered_p" where="prose" />). It answers <Figure id="verity.twoarm_answerable_answered_verity" where="prose" /> questions that have an answer,
      against <Figure id="verity.twoarm_answerable_answered_naive" where="prose" />, and gives <Figure id="verity.twoarm_invented_answer_verity" where="prose" /> fabricated answers, against <Figure
        id="verity.twoarm_invented_answer_naive"
        where="prose"
      />: at this size, neither that cost nor that gain can be told from chance (p = <Figure id="verity.twoarm_answerable_answered_p" where="prose" /> and <Figure
        id="verity.twoarm_invented_answer_p"
        where="prose"
      />). The measured gain is the refusal; the rest cannot be measured on this data.
    </p>
    <MeasuresDetail>
      <p>
        Refusal threshold of the run, which is also the code’s: <Figure
          id="verity.threshold_run_of_record"
          where="detail"
        />.
      </p>
      <MeasuresTable
        where="run"
        caption="Reference run, test questions"
        rows={[
          { id: "verity.invented_answer_rate", reading: "Every answer given, whether the question has an answer or not. One fabrication, named in the results." },
          { id: "verity.refusal_recall", reading: "Questions with no answer in the documents, refused." },
          { id: "verity.refusal_precision", reading: "Refusals that concerned a truly unanswerable question; the other comes from a quote check." },
          { id: "verity.unanswerable_answered", defect: true, reading: "The main defect: an answer given where the documents contain none." },
          { id: "verity.hallucination_rate", reading: "The earlier definition: an answer to an unanswerable question is left out of it. Zero observed is not zero guaranteed: hence the interval." },
          {
            id: "verity.judge_kappa_v020",
            noInterval: "Kappa is not a proportion, and the source file gives no interval for this incomplete check.",
            reading: "The judge of this run, against my blind ratings; its budget stopped it before the end.",
          },
          {
            id: "verity.judge_kappa_all",
            noInterval: "Kappa is not a proportion: its bootstrap interval is in the label.",
            reading: "The judge of the previous runs, real and planted claims.",
          },
          {
            id: "verity.judge_kappa_real",
            noInterval: "Kappa is not a proportion: the source file gives it no useful interval.",
            reading: "Fragile: the human judges almost no real claim unsupported, so a single disagreement moves it a lot.",
          },
          { id: "verity.judge_traps_detected", reading: "Upper bound: a planted claim counts as detected as soon as one of its parts is rejected." },
        ]}
      />
      <MeasuresTable
        where="naive"
        caption="Against a naive RAG, pre-registered comparison (tuning and test questions)"
        ours="Verity"
        baseline="Naive RAG"
        rows={[
          {
            id: "verity.twoarm_unanswerable_answered_verity",
            base: "verity.twoarm_unanswerable_answered_naive",
            label: "Unanswerable questions handled as if they had an answer",
            reading: "Verity’s checks refuse unanswerable questions that the naive version answers: a significant gap after adjustment.",
          },
          {
            id: "verity.twoarm_answerable_answered_verity",
            base: "verity.twoarm_answerable_answered_naive",
            label: "Answerable questions handled",
            reading: "No cost measurable at this sample size.",
          },
          {
            id: "verity.twoarm_invented_answer_verity",
            base: "verity.twoarm_invented_answer_naive",
            label: "Fabricated answers",
            reading: "No difference measurable at this sample size.",
          },
        ]}
      />
      <p>
        An answer that declines by itself counts as a refusal, in both arms: the naive version never refuses, and the comparison would otherwise be won in advance. The spending cap stopped Verity’s test
        run before the end; the comparison covers the questions both finished (<a href={at("scorecards/two-arm-comparison.json")}>comparison file</a>, <a
          href={at("docs/preregistration/two-arm-comparison.md")}>pre-registration</a
        >).
      </p>
    </MeasuresDetail>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection n={4} id="echecs" title="Known limits and next steps" lead="Two limits the figures alone do not show, each with its scope and its next step.">
    <Limit title="The confidence threshold decides nothing">
      <Fragment slot="scope">The confidence rating a model gives each answer, and the threshold below which Verity may refuse.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="limit">
        The shipped threshold, the one of the reference run, is <Figure id="verity.threshold_run_of_record" where="threshold" />: the rule fixed in advance chose it on the tuning questions, where no
        threshold did better than another, and at this value it refuses nothing. Every refusal comes from a check written in code or from the rating model; the confidence rating is the model’s
        self-assessment, and it does not match its actual error rate.
        <blockquote lang="en">
          <p data-verbatim>{thresholdSentence.contains}</p>
          <footer><a href={at("docs/results.md")}>docs/results.md</a></footer>
        </blockquote>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="next">A refusal based on reliable confidence, on the <a href={at("docs/features.md#roadmap")}>roadmap</a> and not claimed while the measurement does not support it.</Fragment>
    </Limit>

    <Limit title="An exact quote of a badly extracted text">
      <Fragment slot="scope">The “word for word” check, which verifies that every quote is in the text extracted from the document.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="limit">
        <blockquote lang="en">
          <p data-verbatim>
            {before}{DEFECT} <span class="sic" lang="en">[sic, PDF extraction defect]</span>{after}
          </p>
          <footer>Quote from the answer to <code>{answer.example_id}</code>, <a href={demoRun}>previous test run</a></footer>
        </blockquote>
        In the <a href={at("datasets/corpus/order_form_northwind_2025.pdf")}>order form PDF</a>, “twenty-” ends a line and “four” starts the next one; text extraction glued the two together without the
        hyphen. The check accepted the quote, which faithfully copies the faulty passage: it proves a quote is in the extracted text, not that this text is faithful to the document.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="next">
        Not scheduled. A rule an audit proposed, refusing any answer with a number missing from its quotes, was measured on the published runs before being switched on: it refused correct answers
        (computed numbers, numbers taken from the question, identifiers, and here a duration written in digits, then in words). It stays off (<a
          href={at("docs/audit/2026-11-03-figure-rule-measurement.md")}>measurement</a
        >).
      </Fragment>
    </Limit>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    n={5}
    id="decisions"
    title="Three decisions"
    lead="Three architecture choices, each written in a decision record (ADR) with its reasons and consequences: enough to judge the reasoning, not only the result."
  >
    <ol class="decisions">
      <li>
        <h3>A refusal is an answer in its own right, and it is measured</h3>
        <p>
          The evaluation set deliberately contains unanswerable questions, and the correctness of refusals is among the main metrics. A change that makes it answer more often by answering wrongly is a
          regression. The record promised a “calibrated” refusal, that is a confidence that matches the actual error rate; the measurement contradicted it, and the record says so at the top.
        </p>
        <p><a href={at("docs/adr/0003-refusal-as-headline-feature.md")}>ADR 0003</a></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>We reproduce the run file, not the model’s output</h3>
        <p>
          No call fixes the model’s randomness. A real run is recorded once, with the code version, the models and the detail of each question, and is never rewritten; every published figure is
          recomputed from that file. Two runs of the same version may differ: that is the model’s randomness, reported as such.
        </p>
        <p><a href={at("docs/adr/0007-reproducibility-from-artefacts.md")}>ADR 0007</a></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Instruction files read locally, without PromptLang</h3>
        <p>
          An earlier record planned to compile the model’s instructions with PromptLang, my other project; that integration was never built. Rather than keep the promise, I wrote down the actual state:
          a small tested file reader, an error if a variable is missing, untrusted content marked as data. The dependency would have served a story, not a need.
        </p>
        <p><a href={at("docs/adr/0005-prompt-files-without-promptlang.md")}>ADR 0005</a></p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection n={6} id="construction" title="How it is built" lead="For a technical reader: the stack, the tools and the models of the reference run.">
    <p>
      Python, FastAPI, Postgres with pgvector (or in-memory storage), local models to compare the meaning of texts and rank the passages, Anthropic and OpenAI clients behind a fallback router,
      Next.js interface. Tooling: uv, ruff, strict mypy, pytest, GitHub Actions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reference run: agent <code>{answer.run.agent_model}</code>, judge <code>{answer.run.judge_model}</code>, meaning comparison <code>{answer.run.embedding_model}</code>. Detailed architecture: <a
        href={at("docs/architecture.md")}>docs/architecture.md</a
      >.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection n={7} id="suite" title="What comes next" lead="What is still missing, according to the project itself.">
    <ul>
      <li>The comparison with the whole corpus placed in the model’s instructions: specified in the pre-registration, not run.</li>
      <li>A refusal based on reliable confidence: the confidence error measured on the test set does not allow it.</li>
      <li>A judge from another model family, and a second human annotator.</li>
      <li>Reading web pages: the code exists, no test exercises it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={at("docs/features.md#roadmap")}>Roadmap</a> · <a href={at("docs/results.md")}>what the figures do not say</a></p>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
